            <ArticleTitle>[Artificial sweeteners and diabetes: friends or foes?].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1246-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Sugary drinks consumption is associated with increased risk of obesity and type 2 diabetes. Thereby, artificial sweeteners (AS) consumption became increasingly popular and were introduced largely in our diet in order to reduce calorie intake and normalise blood glucose levels without altering our taste for &quot;sweetness&quot;. However, the results of published studies on health outcomes secondary to AS intake, including type 2 diabetes risk, are inconsistent. The aim of this article is to focus on the role of AS in glucose homeostasis and diabetes onset.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
